<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_drop_vlabel.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-dropvlabel">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-dropvlabel">
  <primary>DROP VLABEL</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>DROP VLABEL</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>DROP VLABEL</refname>
  <refpurpose>remove a vertex label</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
DROP VLABEL [ IF EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> [, ...] [ CASCADE | RESTRICT ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropvlabel-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>DROP VLABEL</command> removes a vertex label. The table behind it goes too, along
   with its id sequence, its indexes and its constraints. The label is looked up in the graph
   named by <varname>graph_path</varname>, and the name cannot carry the graph. Several names
   may be given in one statement.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>RESTRICT</literal>, which is the default, has two things to object to. A label that
   still holds vertices is refused with
   <literal>cannot drop <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> because it is
   not empty.</literal> That is stricter than the dependency check PostgreSQL applies to a
   table, which does not care how many rows there are. A label that has a child label is
   refused as well, and the message names the child.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> overrides both. It drops the label whatever it holds, and it
   deletes the edges attached to the label's vertices, whatever labels those edges have. The
   edge labels themselves survive, empty. So read <literal>CASCADE</literal> here as "and the
   graph data that depends on it", not only as "and the dependent catalog objects".
  </para>

  <para>
   The base label <literal>ag_vertex</literal> cannot be dropped, and
   <command>DROP VLABEL</command> refuses an edge label; the statement for one is
   <xref linkend="cypher-dropelabel"/>. The table behind a label cannot be dropped on its own
   either: <command>DROP TABLE</command> on it is refused and points here. To empty a graph
   without dropping its labels, write <literal>MATCH (n) DETACH DELETE n</literal> instead.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropvlabel-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>IF EXISTS</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Issue a notice instead of an error for a label that does not exist. Other names in the
      same statement are still dropped.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Name of the vertex label to drop, in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>.
      Several names may be given, separated by commas.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>CASCADE</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Drop the label along with its vertices and its child labels, and delete the edges
      attached to those vertices.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>RESTRICT</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Refuse if the label holds any vertices or has a child label. This is the default.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropvlabel-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>, but build a
   graph of their own and drop it at the end. An empty label goes without comment, because
   nothing else was affected:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH dropvdemo;
SET graph_path = dropvdemo;
CREATE VLABEL film;
CREATE VLABEL person;
CREATE ELABEL directed;
CREATE (:person {name: 'Denis Villeneuve'})-[:directed]-&gt;(:film {title: 'Arrival'});
CREATE VLABEL draft;
DROP VLABEL draft;

DROP VLABEL
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>film</literal> has a vertex in it, so the default refuses:
<programlisting>
DROP VLABEL film;

ERROR:  cannot drop film because it is not empty.
</programlisting>
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> takes the label, its vertex, and the <literal>directed</literal>
   edge that pointed at it. The edge is gone but its label is not:
<programlisting>
DROP VLABEL film CASCADE;
MATCH ()-[e:directed]-&gt;() RETURN count(e) AS directed_edges;

 directed_edges
----------------
 0
(1 row)
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   A label with a child is refused too, and <literal>CASCADE</literal> names the child it takes
   with it:
<programlisting>
CREATE VLABEL work;
CREATE VLABEL documentary INHERITS (work);
DROP VLABEL work;

ERROR:  cannot drop vlabel work because other objects depend on it
DETAIL:  vlabel documentary depends on vlabel work
HINT:  Use DROP ... CASCADE to drop the dependent objects too.
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
DROP VLABEL work CASCADE;

NOTICE:  drop cascades to vlabel documentary
DROP VLABEL
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Several labels can be named at once, and <literal>IF EXISTS</literal> passes over the ones
   that are already gone:
<programlisting>
CREATE VLABEL a;
CREATE VLABEL b;
DROP VLABEL IF EXISTS a, b, c;

NOTICE:  vlabel "c" does not exist, skipping
DROP VLABEL
</programlisting>
  </para>

  <para>
   Refusals worth recognizing. Each statement drops one kind of label, the base labels stay, and
   the table cannot be dropped out from under the label:
<programlisting>
DROP ELABEL person;

ERROR:  DROP ELABEL cannot DROP vertex label
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
DROP VLABEL ag_vertex;

ERROR:  DROP base vertex label is prohibited
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
DROP TABLE dropvdemo.person;

ERROR:  cannot drop table dropvdemo.person because vlabel person requires it
HINT:  You can drop vlabel person instead.
</programlisting>
   <literal>DROP GRAPH dropvdemo CASCADE</literal> drops the graph the examples built.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropvlabel-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   An edge whose endpoint vertex is deleted is deleted with it, both here and in
   <literal>DETACH DELETE</literal>. <structfield>start</structfield> and
   <structfield>end</structfield> are plain <type>graphid</type> columns rather than foreign
   keys, so it is the graph write path that does this, not a referential action on the table.
  </para>

  <para>
   <varname>cypher_allow_unsafe_ddl</varname> switches off the kind check and the base label
   check, so with it on <command>DROP ELABEL</command> will drop a vertex label and
   <literal>ag_vertex</literal> can be dropped. It exists to repair a graph whose catalog is
   already wrong. Leave it off otherwise. See <xref linkend="agensgraph-config"/>.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-dropvlabel-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no statement that drops a label. Element types belong to a graph type,
   which is dropped whole by the drop graph type statement of clause 12.7, and dropping one
   deletes no data.
  </para>

  <para>
   <command>DROP VLABEL</command> corresponds to <command>DROP TABLE</command>, and
   <literal>IF EXISTS</literal>, the name list and <literal>CASCADE</literal> or
   <literal>RESTRICT</literal> behave as they do there. Two things have no
   <command>DROP TABLE</command> counterpart. A label that is not empty is refused unless
   <literal>CASCADE</literal> is written, where <command>DROP TABLE</command> does not care how
   many rows a table has. And <literal>CASCADE</literal> also deletes edges that live in other
   labels, which is graph data rather than a dependent catalog object.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

